bitcoin about 12 years ago got started  on halloween of 2008 the bitcoin white  paper was published on january 3rd 2009  bitcoin was put out into the world at  that time it was a crazy crazy idea in  the corner of the internet no way  could this insane idea ever get any  traction if you fast forward 12 years we  are now at over a trillion dollar market  cap every single financial institution  in the entire world is trying to figure  out what their bitcoin strategy is  there's over a hundred million  individuals that hold bitcoin personally  the average exposure is about four  thousand dollars when you start to look  at corporations like microstrategy tesla  and square they have it on their balance  sheet there's plenty of bitcoin miners  that are publicly traded lots of other  investment firms like galaxy digital et  cetera that also bitcoin another balance  sheet  bitcoin has grown up but the one corner  of the financial system where bitcoin  still has not penetrated it has not been  able to weasel its way in is the banks  and the banks always hide behind  regulation they say we are regulated  entities we cannot participate in this  we're not allowed to those that oversee  us will not let us hold bitcoin hold or  custody bitcoin for our customers or  participate in the various financial  services around bitcoin and other  cryptocurrencies but that might change  now the fdic chairman jelena mcwilliams  has just come out yesterday and said  that banks are now  potentially going to be able to custody  and use these assets this is a very very  big deal so first let's talk about the  fdic the fdic when you go to their  website you say what is the fdic it says  the federal deposit insurance  corporation is an independent agency  created by congress to maintain  stability and public confidence in the  nation's financial system it's all about  stability and public confidence to  accomplish this mission the fdic insures  deposits it examines and supervises  financial institutions for safety  soundness and consumer protection and it  makes large and complex financial  institutions resolvable and manages  receiverships so basically the fdic says  hey yo banks you guys do your job if you  don't do your job we're here we got your  back we're going to provide federal  deposit insurance that's the whole point  of the fdic  now you may not know about the fdic that  since its creation in 1933 according to  its website they have been an essential  part of the american financial system in  the 1920s and early 1930s a rise in bank  failures created a national crisis  wiping out many american savings  since the fdic insurance began in 1934  no depositor has lost a single penny of  insured funds due to bank failure so in  the 20s and 30s there was all kinds of  issues banks were failing and people  would quite literally lose their money  if you had a hundred dollars there the  bank failed were out of luck done  fdic stepped in and said we will insure  let's say retail bank accounts up to 250  000 so if you had 250 000 in a bank  account since 1934 to today you've never  lost it because it is insured by the  fdic  very big deal now how many banks are  there the best business show research  team we started to dig into this and try  to understand just how big are the banks  well there are over 4 000 commercial  banks and over 600 saving institutions  so about 5 000 total institutions that  fall under the fdic  they hold approximately 22 trillion  dollars in assets  when we talk about the financial system  the banks or the big boys  22 trillion dollars in assets about 5  000 institutions so if all of a sudden  the fdic is able to come up with  regulation and rules that allows these 5  000 institutions to get in the game it  would be a very very big deal you would  see a massive increase in demand and for  something like bitcoin that has a fixed  supply if there is a massive increase in  demand and a fixed supply asset the us  dollar price has to appreciate to  accommodate everyone it's just simple  supply and demand economics 101. so we  go ahead and we look at what jolena  mcwilliams stated the first quote is if  we don't bring this activity inside the  banks it is going to develop outside of  the banks the federal regulators won't  be able to regulate it and so she's  clearly stating that it is obvious that  the retail users the investors the  depositors they want this product this  technology has found product market fit  and that activity is only going to  continue and it will continue inside or  outside of the regulator's purview  if they do not act and so therefore if  the fdic and regulators decide to come  up with a rule set so that banks can get  in the game  then that activity or some portion of it  will start to actually permeate into the  banks and there will be lots of progress  and activities that the banks can  participate in so this is very  forward-thinking in my opinion because  you have the chairman of the fdic saying  this is real people want it and it is  not going to reverse the trend will not  go backwards it is only going to go  forward from here  we can choose to participate or to sit  this one out but if we have 5 000  institutions and 22 trillion dollars of  assets and there is a brand new  financial system being built a brand new  financial asset that we have no ability  to interface with we should probably  change that if we then go and we look at  the second quote she said the issue  there and she's specifically talking  about banks being able to have bitcoin  holdings for client trading collateral  for loans or held as assets on their  balance sheet so the issue there is  valuation of these assets and the  fluctuation in their value can be almost  on a daily basis you have to decide what  kind of capital and liquidity treatment  to allocate to such balance sheet  holdings so the whole idea here would  not just be for the bank to invest in  bitcoin what they're specifically  talking about is for the banks to go  ahead and gather bitcoin to  convert cash into bitcoin put it on  their balance sheet and then to use the  bitcoin on their balance sheet for  trading for their clients to allow  clients to buy and sell bitcoin to allow  it to serve as collateral for loans  again bitcoin is the most pristine  collateral in the world  and it will also allow the banks to  simply hold it on their balance sheet so  it's not just about the financial gain  of the banks but it's also about  allowing bitcoin to serve as an asset in  financial services this is ultimately  how bitcoin goes from a simply an  investable global store value to  actually being incorporated into the  global financial system in a way that  most people have thought would not  happen  now if we go ahead and we think about  how they are doing this we obviously  have the chairwoman of the fdic spending  a ton of time publicly stating this and  saying we are working on back in may  there was something called a sprint team  that sprint team was announced in may  and it has the goal of ensuring that  crypto policy coordination among three  bank regulators the fdic is one of them  the federal reserve is the second and  the office of the comptroller of the  currency the occ is the third so what  you now have is not just the fdic  chairperson saying hey we want to get  banks in the game we're trying to figure  out how to do that but we also can go  back to may and see that they put  together a team to get the fdic the  federal reserve and the occ all on board  to get fixed in the game  i don't know what the rules will end up  being i don't know what the timeline is  for them to get there but it is obvious  to me now that the banks will get into  bitcoin and as we know again whether  you're an individual you're a  corporation you're a financial  institution or you're a nation state  bitcoin is good for business and the  banks have been sitting on the sidelines  and they do not want to sit there  